Something that's not quite clearly explained in win/X11/Install.X11:
Once you've installed the nh10 font, you need to redefine nethack's
output symbols to use the funky characters in the nh10 font.  The file
win/X11/nethack.rc explains this more.

(To make sure the nh10 font is properly installed, try running
"xfd -font nh10".)

I tried using the definitions in the sample nethack.rc for a little
while, but I can't seem to parse the pictures right; they don't have
the right visual associations.  Part of the problem is nethack is
still a little too closely coupled to its ascii representation.  For
example: the picture for gems is a faceted gem, but in reality, most
of the time you're just looking at a pile of rocks.

Part of this is a flaw of the pictures: the rocks/gems symbol could be
more like a rock or an uncut gem, and that would parse better.

But it would be nice if you had finer-grain control of output
representation.  Each subclass of armor or tool could use its own
symbol.
